WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.420
Automatic objects are useful precisely because their lifetime is simple: they exist while execution is within the relevant block or function.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.680
The danger appears when a pointer to such an object escapes that scope.

3
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:19.260
Once the scope ends, the object no longer exists, even if a copied pointer value remains somewhere in the program.

4
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:27.940
Returning the address of a local object is a classic lifetime error because the caller receives a pointer to storage that has already ended its lifetime.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:39.400
The same problem can appear less obviously when code stores a local address in a global, a heap object, a callback context, a work queue, or a structure that outlives the function call.

6
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:48.400
Short-lived addresses should not be placed into longer-lived data structures unless the longer-lived structure is only borrowing the pointer during a known valid-use window.

7
00:00:48.500 --> 00:01:00.820
If a structure needs the value after the local object is gone, it should copy the data, receive caller-provided storage with a clear lifetime, or take ownership of dynamically allocated storage where appropriate.

8
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.020
Callbacks and asynchronous code deserve special attention.

9
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:12.480
A pointer that is safe during a synchronous call may not be safe after the function returns or after another thread continues work.

10
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:19.140
The API should say whether a pointer is consumed immediately, retained, copied, or merely observed.

11
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:25.500
A good review question is whether every retained pointer remains valid for as long as the retaining code can use it.

12
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.360
If the answer depends on a local variable that has gone out of scope, the lifetime contract is wrong.

13
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.740
Safer code either keeps the lifetime local or makes ownership explicit.
